{
  "dom_id": "step-q30324",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A $60\\,\\text{in}$ tall runner is training at night in a park. While running past a lamppost that is $100\\,\\text{in}$ tall, she notices her shadow is growing. Her watch tells her that she is travelling at $50\\,\\text{in/s}.$ How fast is the length of her shadow changing while she runs away from the lamppost?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/4ef1ba4975846a5375a18dee6cb4a55ba84d22d7c98b19eeaeebbb6b0017b623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the runner's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the person's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the runner's velocity is positive because she is running away from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the runner and the lamp increases with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{100} & =\\frac{y}{60} \\\\ 60x+60y & =100y \\\\ 60x & =40y \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (50) \\\\  & =\\frac{150}{2} \\\\  & =75\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the runner's shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{in/s}.$",
